A51 — lay the scan-out image out at the pool buffer's row stride

A scan-out image is allocated from the session's pool, and that buffer is
gralloc's: its rows are padded for widths gralloc does not like (1024 px ->
5120 B, 1440 px -> 5888 B on Adreno), which is the same fact the ICD already
handles for its own dma-buf slots and export-emulated images. The image here is
created by gamescope through a DRM-format-modifier *list*, so Turnip picks the
layout, and for a LINEAR modifier it picks width * 4. The compositor then reads
the buffer at the buffer's stride and the GPU wrote it at the driver's: every
row lands a little further along than the row read back, so the frame comes out
sheared, repeated across the width, with the tail of the buffer unwritten.

Only a composited frame touches this image. A frame that direct-scans-out
(gamescope's `drm_prepare` path: one screen-sized layer, no filter that needs
the shader, no look, no ReShade) is the guest's own buffer, so it is unaffected
-- which is why the fault appears exactly when a scaling filter (NEAREST, FSR,
NIS, SGSR) forces `vulkan_composite`, and not before.

At 1280 and 1920 wide the stride is tight, so the two pitches agree and no
output this stack has run before could show it.

The pitch is not derivable from the geometry, so it is asked for: the shim owns
the pool and answers `gamenative_drm_scanout_pitch(w, h)` -- measuring it with
one throwaway pool allocation for a geometry it has not seen. The output image
is then created with an explicit LINEAR modifier at that stride instead of the
modifier list, which also makes the AddFB2 export report the same stride the
compositor reads. A real KMS session has no shim, the weak symbol does not
resolve, and the driver's own choice stands.

